(>> 5.1 Lubrication Mechanisms and Principles) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(A lubricant is any substance introduced between two moving surfaces in relative contact) Tj
T*
(to reduce friction, minimize wear, and dissipate frictional heat.) Tj
T*
(* Mechanisms of Lubrication:) Tj
T*
(1. Thick-Film \(Hydrodynamic / Fluid Film\) Lubrication: Moving surfaces are completely) Tj
T*
(separated by a continuous, thick fluid lubricant film \(~1,000 A\). Frictional resistance) Tj
T*
(arises solely from internal fluid shear viscosity: F = eta * A * \(dv/dy\). Operating in) Tj
T*
(high-speed, light-to-moderate load systems \(e.g., sewing machines, hydrodynamic journal) Tj
T*
(bearings\).) Tj
T*
(2. Thin-Film \(Boundary\) Lubrication: Under high loads or low rotational speeds, the) Tj
T*
(hydrodynamic film squeezes out. Metal asperities come into near-contact, separated only) Tj
T*
(by a chemically or physically adsorbed molecular monolayer \(thickness ~1-2 molecules\).) Tj
T*
(Governed by polar lubricants \(fatty acids, vegetable oils\) whose polar headgroups anchor) Tj
T*
(to metal surfaces.) Tj
T*
(3. Extreme-Pressure \(EP\) Lubrication: Under severe operating loads and high friction) Tj
T*
(temperatures \(>200 deg C\), boundary monolayers desorb. EP additives containing active) Tj
T*
(chemical compounds of Sulfur, Chlorine, or Phosphorus react with metal surfaces to form) Tj
T*
(inorganic solid crystalline films \(iron sulfide, iron chloride, iron phosphide\) with low) Tj
T*
(shear strength, preventing catastrophic seizure and welding.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 5.2 Physical and Chemical Properties of Lubricating Oils) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Viscosity and Viscosity Index \(VI\): Viscosity measures internal resistance to flow.) Tj
T*
(Viscosity Index \(Dean & Davis Scale\) measures the rate of change of viscosity with) Tj
T*
(temperature:) Tj
T*
(VI = [ \(L - U\) / \(L - H\) ] * 100, where U = viscosity of test oil at 40 deg C, L =) Tj
T*
(viscosity of zero-VI oil at 40 deg C, and H = viscosity of 100-VI oil at 40 deg C. High) Tj
T*
(VI indicates minimal viscosity change across broad temperature ranges.) Tj
T*
(* Flash Point and Fire Point: Flash point is the lowest temperature at which an oil) Tj
T*
(gives off sufficient vapor to ignite momentarily when an open flame is applied. Fire) Tj
T*
(point is the temperature at which vapors sustain continuous combustion for at least 5) Tj
T*
(seconds \(typically 5-40 deg C above flash point\). Determines operational fire safety.) Tj
T*
(* Cloud Point and Pour Point: Cloud point is the temperature at which paraffin waxes) Tj
T*
(crystallize, giving a cloudy appearance. Pour point is the lowest temperature at) Tj
T*
(which the oil continues to pour or flow when tilted horizontally. Crucial for) Tj
T*
(sub-zero machinery operation.) Tj
T*
(* Saponification Value and Acid Number: Saponification value is the mg of KOH) Tj
T*
(required to saponify 1 g of oil \(detects animal/vegetable oil blending in mineral) Tj
T*
(oils\). Acid number is the mg of KOH required to neutralize free fatty acids in 1 g of) Tj
T*
(oil.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
