(>> 5.3 Refractories and Portland Cement) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Refractories: Heat-resistant inorganic materials capable of withstanding extreme) Tj
T*
(furnace temperatures \(>1,580 deg C\) without deforming or melting:) Tj
T*
(- Refractoriness \(Pyrometric Cone Equivalent PCE\): Tested using Seger cones.) Tj
T*
(- Refractoriness Under Load \(RUL\): Temperature at which a refractory specimen) Tj
T*
(collapses under 2.0 kg/cm^2 compressive load.) Tj
T*
(* Portland Cement: Hydraulic binder consisting of: Tricalcium silicate \(C3S, 45-60%,) Tj
T*
(early strength\), Dicalcium silicate \(C2S, 15-30%, progressive ultimate strength\),) Tj
T*
(Tricalcium aluminate \(C3A, 6-12%, fast flash setting\), and Tetracalcium) Tj
T*
(aluminoferrite \(C4AF, 6-8%\).) Tj
T*
(- Setting and Hardening Reactions: Hydration forms tobermorite gel \(calcium) Tj
T*
(silicate hydrate C-S-H\) and crystalline Ca\(OH\)2. Gypsum \(CaSO4.2H2O, 2-3%\) is) Tj
T*
(added during clinker grinding to retard flash setting by forming insoluble calcium) Tj
T*
(sulfoaluminate \(ettringite\).) Tj
T*
