T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([SOLVED PROBLEM 1: Viscosity Index Determination via Dean and Davis Equation]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: A lubricating oil has a kinematic viscosity of 62.0 cSt at 40 deg C and 8.0 cSt) Tj
T*
(at 100 deg C. The reference oils having identical viscosity at 100 deg C \(8.0 cSt\)) Tj
T*
(exhibit viscosities at 40 deg C of: L = 115.0 cSt \(Gulf oil, VI = 0\) and H = 55.0 cSt) Tj
T*
(\(Pennsylvanian oil, VI = 100\). Calculate the Viscosity Index \(VI\) of the test oil.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Identify Parameters:) Tj
T*
(U \(Viscosity of test oil at 40 deg C\) = 62.0 cSt.) Tj
T*
(L \(Viscosity of 0-VI standard oil at 40 deg C\) = 115.0 cSt.) Tj
T*
(H \(Viscosity of 100-VI standard oil at 40 deg C\) = 55.0 cSt.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Apply Dean and Davis Formula:) Tj
T*
(VI = [ \(L - U\) / \(L - H\) ] * 100) Tj
T*
(L - U = 115.0 - 62.0 = 53.0 cSt.) Tj
T*
(L - H = 115.0 - 55.0 = 60.0 cSt.) Tj
T*
(VI = [ 53.0 / 60.0 ] * 100 = 0.8833 * 100 = 88.33.) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Viscosity Index is 88.33, demonstrating stable thermal viscosity retention) Tj
T*
(suitable for automotive engines.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([SOLVED PROBLEM 2: Saponification Value Determination]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: 2.50 g of a blended lubricating oil was refluxed with 25.0 mL of 0.5 N) Tj
T*
(ethanolic KOH. The unreacted KOH required 12.0 mL of 0.5 N HCl in a back-titration. A) Tj
T*
(blank titration without oil required 24.5 mL of 0.5 N HCl. Calculate the Saponification) Tj
T*
(Value of the oil.) Tj
T*
